] [17592186507377 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e28d2851-cb92-5497-aeb3-e6113495aa58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507393 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186506703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507393 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507393 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507393 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507393 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507393 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507393 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507393 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8a63a77-7e75-5948-b4a4-f7660df42d29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507448 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507448 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507448 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507448 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507448 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507448 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "735d1714-8711-57ac-8156-7342afc0beb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507448 #edb/ref 1074 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{9{\\pi }^{2}}=3\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $(L=1)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{1}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=3\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))+x(-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi A=0 \\\\ 6\\pi B=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{54\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507450 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507450 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507450 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507450 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}+49y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507450 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "776f8284-bdba-5ab6-8f5c-16f7e1be5e32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507450 #edb/ref 1074 "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+49({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients, we obtain \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507482 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507482 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343874" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507482 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507482 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-4y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507482 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af83bf7a-2326-5550-86bc-d86a83bd79bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507482 #edb/ref 1074 "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)-4({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine coefficients, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{b}_{n}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)=\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507508 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507508 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507508 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343914" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507508 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507508 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507508 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d683633f-d60f-57e1-b38c-1931a877a7fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507508 #edb/ref 1074 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A=-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=\\frac{9}{16\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507537 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186507557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507537 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1e364e0b-e003-503e-aadb-895d96ad9926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507537 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186368362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507540 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186507552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507540 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "29506e54-5d8b-54a1-b9a6-9e847bc7683d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507540 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186507450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507552 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186507537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507552 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "58ba9283-6ba3-5bb3-8a8e-3db7151c4592" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507552 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186507448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507557 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7136d128-593a-539d-a5b4-9cc5e8e074f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507557 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186507508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507577 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186507540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507577 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3262fec-399e-5719-a5fb-ab0190064e6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507577 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186507482 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
